  "title": "Recognizing Social Engineering Patterns",
  "content": "Social engineering is the use of deception and human interaction to make someone reveal information, grant access, transfer value, or bypass a normal safeguard. The request may arrive by email, text, phone, social media, chat, or in person. The channel changes; the underlying pattern does not.\n\n<b>Look beyond one message</b>\n\nMost attempts combine a believable identity, a plausible reason, and pressure to act before verification. An attacker may impersonate a help desk, coworker, vendor, authority figure, or community member. Professional language, familiar branding, and accurate background details make the story more convincing, but none of them proves identity.\n\nA coordinated campaign may use several channels at once. A text can introduce a false problem, a cloned account can reinforce it, and a call can offer the supposed solution. Repeated names, domains, phrases, timing, targets, or campaign markers help defenders connect reports that initially look unrelated. Treat those shared indicators as leads, not as permission to interact with suspicious systems.\n\n<b>Identify the requested exception</b>\n\nAsk what the sender wants and which normal safeguard the request would bypass. Common goals include opening an unverified link, disclosing an authentication code, installing software, changing account settings, transferring money, sharing internal information, or allowing physical entry. Requests become especially risky when the sender discourages an independent check or claims that normal procedure does not apply.\n\n<b>Verify outside the conversation</b>\n\nContact the person or organization through a known route rather than a number, account, or link supplied in the message. Confirm both identity and authorization; a real employee can still make an unsafe request. Preserve the original contact and report it through the approved channel.\n\nPeople targeted by social engineering are not the problem. Attackers deliberately exploit normal qualities such as helpfulness, trust, curiosity, and respect for authority. A strong response protects the person involved and improves the process the attacker tried to abuse.",
  "links": [
    "social engineering",
    "cloned help desk",
    "impersonation"
  ]
}
